.\" conn(1) is written by hand in man(7) troff. The guide at
.\" https://conn.w0zro.com/guide tells the same story in walking order;
.\" this page is the reference. Keep the two agreeing.
.TH CONN 1 2026-09-02 conn "User Commands"
.SH NAME
conn \- a terminal UI for working on projects at the command line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B conn
.br
.B conn ls
.br
.B conn restart
.br
.B conn
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help | \-\-version ]
.br
.B conn
.RB [ help | version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B conn
shows every git repository under your project roots, what is running in
each, and the shells you open there. The shells are panes in a
.BR tmux (1)
server of conn's own, and
.B conn
is a tmux client: it brings that server up under its own configuration,
attaches the terminal to it, and runs the navigator down the left of the
session's home window. The shell under the navigator's cursor is the pane
beside it, drawn by tmux, which takes the keys to it; the rest wait in
windows of their own. The navigator is where the shells are listed,
found, started and killed. Close the terminal, come back, and everything
is where it was.
.PP
There is nothing to set up beyond having tmux and
.BR lsof (8)
installed \(em the process list is read through lsof. The
configuration is conn's, written at every launch into the state directory
and sourced into a server already running, so no .tmux.conf reaches the
server and every window has this build's keys. By default conn looks for
repositories under
.IR ~/projects ;
the config points it elsewhere.
.PP
The navigator's left column is a tree, at most four levels deep: group,
repository, sub\-project, process. A folder holding two or more
repositories groups them. In a monorepo, a directory with a manifest
.RI ( .conn ,
.IR Procfile ,
.IR package.json ,
.IR go.mod ,
.IR Cargo.toml ,
and the like) is a project of its own. Processes are attributed to the
innermost place they belong to and named by what you would call them \(em
.BR "npm run dev" ,
not
.BR node .
Dim rows are processes with no shell of their own to go to: those
started outside conn, and those a process in a shell started in turn
\(em a claude's tools, say \(em which enter reaches only as far as the
shell above them. All are listed and killable. One of them may be conn itself \(em the launcher
become a tmux client, in the terminal it was started from \(em and that
row says
.B (me)
in a color of its own. The pane beside the list is the shell under the
cursor, live; on a row with no shell the navigator has the window, and
the pane says what is known about the row.
.PP
.B conn ls
is the same server's state for a reader that is not a person: the held
shells, one per line on stdout \(em pid, directory and name,
tab\-separated, the name empty for a shell opened by hand. No server
listening means nothing is held, which is an empty list, not an error.
.PP
.B conn restart
is
.B R
and then
.BR conn ,
typed at a terminal outside the window: the server ended with every
shell it holds \(em after asking, when it holds any \(em and brought up
again under this build's configuration, with the terminal attached. It
is the way to a fresh server for a raised
.BR scrollback ,
or on the day something wedges. From inside the window it refuses,
since the pane running it would go with the server; that is what
.B R
is for. No server running is nothing to end, and the window opens the
way it does on a first launch.
.SH KEYS
.B ?
shows this list inside conn, in a popup over the whole window \(em from
the navigator, or with the prefix from any shell; the next key puts it
away. The status line at the foot of the terminal is tmux's. It begins
with conn's own name,
.BR CONN ,
in purple, which stays; to its right is the mode the keys are in:
.B NAV
or
.B PROC
for the pane that has them, each a chip in its own color with the line
washed one tone after it,
.B PREFIX
while a chord hangs,
.B COPY
in copy mode, the query while one is typed and the filter while one
stands,
.B CONTINUE
with the picker open, and
.B CONFIRM
while a kill waits on its second key. Then anything conn has to say \(em
a report, an error, a confirmation \(em until the next key; what a chord
says, from any shell, shows there too, for a few seconds.
.SS In the navigator
.TP
.B "j k"
move; the arrows move too
.TP
.B "gg G"
jump to the top and the bottom
.TP
.B "J K"
show the next and previous shell, in the list's order, with the keys in
it
.TP
.B s
open a shell at the selected place, beside the navigator, with the keys
in it
.TP
.B a
start an agent there \(em Claude Code, unless the config's
.B agent
says otherwise
.TP
.B A
continue a suspended conversation there: the place's past agent sessions,
newest first and searchable
.TP
.B r
run the plan's entries that are not already running, each in a window of
its own
.TP
.B enter
the keys to the selected shell, beside the list
.TP
.B tab
go to the next agent waiting on you; again continues around them
.TP
.B space
fold or unfold the selected node
.TP
.B \-
fold or unfold everything; unfolded rows also show pids
.TP
.B .
toggle between everything and only what is running
.TP
.B /
find by name: repositories, and the processes whose commands answer
.TP
.B esc
clear a standing filter
.TP
.B x
kill the selected process; on a repository or group, everything running
in it
.TP
.B X
kill the selected process and everything under it
.TP
.B R
end the server outright, and every shell it holds with it
.TP
.B q
leave: detach the terminal. The shells keep running, and the navigator
keeps its place for the next
.B conn
.PP
Every kill asks first \(em
.BR x ,
.B y
or
.B enter
confirms, anything else cancels. Processes get SIGTERM, and the list
never claims an exit it has not observed.
.SS In the filter
Letters type, and the list narrows \(em each word of the query has to
appear in order inside a name, a path or a command, not contiguously,
so
.B tsl
finds tressle\-api and
.B mono api
finds the api inside mono, with the letters that matched lit in the
rows \(em under a cursor that
.B up
and
.B down
(or
.B ctrl\-p
and
.BR ctrl\-n )
move. A query lists the processes whose commands answer it as well as the
places, pruned to the branches that answer; an empty query lists places
alone. Acting on what the cursor is on ends the looking:
.B enter
opens a shell on a place and goes to a found process's window,
.B ctrl\-r
runs the plan there,
.B ctrl\-a
starts an agent,
.B tab
goes to the next waiting agent, and
.B ctrl\-x
asks to kill \(em the filter holds, so the subject stays listed.
.B esc
abandons the look, putting the cursor back on the row it left. The query
is a line like any other: it takes readline's editing keys \(em
.BR ctrl\-w ,
.BR ctrl\-u ,
.BR ctrl\-h ,
.B ctrl\-e
and the rest \(em except the chords above, which are conn's.
.SS In a shell
Every keystroke is the shell's; the pane is tmux's, and it behaves the
way a tmux pane behaves, with the navigator beside it. conn keeps one key:
.TP
.B ctrl\-space
the prefix \(em tmux's own, bound by conn's configuration; it reaches conn
from any shell
.PP
The mouse is tmux's, with tmux's own bindings and nothing of conn's: the
wheel scrolls back through a pane's transcript, a drag selects and copies
to the clipboard on release, a click takes the keys to the pane under it,
and a program that speaks mouse gets its own clicks and wheel. Hold shift
to drag a selection the terminal's own way. A program that copies for
itself \(em vim's clipboard yank, anything speaking OSC 52 \(em reaches
the system clipboard through tmux.
.SS The chords
Each hangs off
.B ctrl\-space
and keeps its letter's meaning; every key tmux would otherwise bind is
unbound.
.TP
.B \-
to the navigator, from any shell
.TP
.B "j k"
the next and previous shell, in the navigator's order
.TP
.B ctrl\-space
again \(em between the navigator and the shell
.TP
.B enter
the next agent waiting on you; again continues around them
.TP
.B /
the navigator, looking
.TP
.B "s a r"
a shell, an agent, the plan \(em where the keys are, by the current
pane's directory
.TP
.B A
the navigator, with the picker open
.TP
.B v
read back: tmux's copy mode over the transcript, with vi keys \(em
.B v
marks,
.B y
copies to the clipboard,
.B q
leaves
.TP
.B q
leave, from anywhere
.TP
.B R
end the server and every shell it holds, after asking
.TP
.B ?
the keys, over everything
.PP
Each shell keeps 10,000 lines of transcript unless the config says
otherwise.
.SH AGENTS
.B a
starts an agent in a shell like any other. conn knows agents as kinds
\(em claude and ollama today \(em and each shows only what its tool
advertises. Claude Code advertises everything; conn reads the session
files it leaves behind, and marks each instance in the navigator: a
spinner while it works, a diamond (\[u25C6]) when it is blocked mid\-turn on
a permission prompt or a question, a filled dot (\[u25CF]) when it has
finished its turn, a hollow dot (\[u25CB]) when it has been idle since it
started. The diamond is the one worth crossing the room for: answering it
resumes work already underway.
.B ctrl\-space enter
goes to the next agent waiting on you, from any shell; pressed again it
continues around the waiting ones in turn.
.PP
A conversation does not end when its instance exits; it is suspended,
and the transcript Claude keeps is enough to pick it back up.
.B A
on a place lists what is suspended there \(em including its
sub\-projects, and for a group its repositories \(em newest first: when
each conversation last moved, the branch it was on, and the last thing
asked of it. Letters narrow the list the way the filter narrows,
.B up
and
.B down
move,
.B enter
continues the one under the cursor \(em a shell opens where the
conversation was had, running
.BR "claude --resume" ,
and from there it is a live instance like any other \(em and
.B esc
closes the list. A conversation an instance is still carrying is not
offered.
.PP
A kind that advertises nothing \(em an ollama REPL \(em is still an
agent to start: its rows are plain processes, unmarked. Ollama runs the
model most recently pulled or run, read from the manifests it keeps
under
.I ~/.ollama/models
(or
.BR $OLLAMA_MODELS ),
so the pick needs no daemon; the REPL itself does, and says so in the
shell when it is not up. With nothing pulled,
.B a
runs
.B ollama list
there instead, which says the true thing.
.SH PLANS
A project writes down its usual processes in
.IR .conn ,
Procfile format, a name and a command per line:
.PP
.EX
dev:   npm run dev
api:   go run ./cmd/api
agent: claude
.EE
.PP
Without one, a
.I Procfile
means every line is a process, and a
.I package.json
contributes its
.B dev
or
.B start
script, whichever exists first.
.B r
starts the entries that are not running, each in its own window named for
its entry. It is a list to run, not a supervisor: nothing restarts on its
own.
.SH CONFIGURATION
One JSON file, entirely optional, at
.IR ~/.config/conn/config.json .
.TP
.B projectsDir
the directory searched for repositories (default
.IR $HOME/projects );
.B ~
and
.B $VARS
expand
.TP
.B projectsDirs
several roots; when set,
.B projectsDir
is ignored. A root that only exists on another machine is fine.
.TP
.B scrollback
transcript lines each shell keeps (default 10000). Written into the
server's configuration at launch, so raising it takes a fresh server
\(em
.B R
and then
.BR conn ,
or
.B conn restart
\(em to reach anything.
.TP
.B navWidth
the navigator column's width, 16\(en60 (default 30)
.TP
.B theme
the side tmux draws for: "dark" (the default) or "light". The navigator
draws with the terminal's own sixteen colors and needs no telling; the
status line, the borders and the popups are tmux's, which takes colors
rather than slots, so the config says which side.
.TP
.B agent
the kind of agent
.B a
starts:
.B claude
(the default) or
.BR ollama .
A name conn does not know falls back to the default
.TP
.B agentRuns
what starting a kind runs, by name, when its own answer is not the
wanted one \(em
.B {"ollama": "ollama run mistral"}
picks the model ollama would otherwise guess at (the one most recently
pulled or run)
.TP
.B skipDirs
directory names the repository scan never enters, on top of the built\-in
list
.RI ( node_modules ,
.IR vendor ,
.IR target ,
and the like)
.SH THE SERVER
The shells live in a tmux server of conn's own: one server per user, for
all projects, on a socket private to conn. Every
.B conn
writes the server's configuration \(em the prefix and its chords, the
status line, the transcript cap, the rest \(em into the state directory,
brings the server up under it or sources it into one already running, and
attaches. The session's first window is the home window: the navigator,
.BR "conn nav" ,
in the pane down its left, and the shell under the navigator's cursor in
the pane beside it. Moving the cursor to another shell's row moves that
shell in and the last one out to a window of its own, so the shells not
shown wait in windows; on a row with no shell the navigator has the whole
window. The navigator's pane is as wide as the config's
.BR navWidth ,
through every resize. Closing the navigator is fine, and
.B ctrl\-space \-
makes it again, on the left. A
.B conn
from a newer build than the navigator's restarts the navigator in its
pane, the way it re\-reads the configuration: the server keeps the
shells, and everything else is the build's. Run from a shell inside the
window, it does that and no more, the terminal being attached already.
Inside any other tmux it refuses: attaching would be nesting.
.PP
The chords run
.B conn
itself with a word \(em
.BR home ,
.BR shell ,
.BR agent ,
.BR run ,
.BR jump ,
.BR next ,
.BR prev ,
.B keys
\(em each a short command against the server; the navigator's pane runs
.B conn nav
and the keys popup
.BR "conn page" .
None of them are for typing.
.PP
The server lives exactly as long as it holds something: the home window
brings it up, the last window closing takes it down. Upgrading conn never
disturbs it \(em the server is not conn's binary, and the next
.B conn
sources the new build's configuration into the same server. A fresh
server, when one is wanted, is
.B R
and then
.BR conn ,
or
.B conn restart
from a terminal outside the window. Anything tmux
can do to the server, you can do to it:
.EX
tmux -S ~/.local/state/conn/tmux-<uid>.sock attach
.EE
is what
.B conn
does.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/conn/config.json
configuration; see
.B CONFIGURATION
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/conn/tmux\-<uid>.sock
the socket of the tmux server holding the shells
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/conn/tmux.conf
the server's configuration, written by conn at every launch
.TP
.I .conn
a project's plan; see
.B PLANS
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CONN_SOCKET
where the server listens, instead of the state directory
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
where the config lives, instead of
.I ~/.config
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
where the server's socket and configuration live, instead of
.I ~/.local/state
.TP
.B SHELL
the shell opened for
.BR s ,
and the one waiting behind
.BR a ,
.B A
and
.B r
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
set, the navigator draws without color, as
.UR https://no\-color.org
the convention
.UE
asks.
.B CLICOLOR_FORCE
and
.B TERM=dumb
are honored too.
.TP
.B CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR
where Claude Code's session files are read from, instead of
.I ~/.claude
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on a clean exit, 1 when conn or its server fails, 2 for an argument it
does not understand.
